l soprano voice and often sang  in the high masses of their church as a  little child he sat on the organ bench  next to his father who was the church  organist and indeed in time Bruckner  would grow up to be the most famous  organist of the late 19th century in  Europe sadly at age 12 his father died  and his mother realizing what a gifted  child he was was very eager for him to  get an excellent education so she placed  him in st. florian which was a monastery  that ran a school for boys and that had  a boy's choir for the masses and the  liturgies that took place there so  that's where anton bruckner grew up and  he loved it there again and again  throughout his life whenever he got over  extended he went back to st. Florian's  it was for him what Jerusalem was for  st. Paul it was the center of spiritual  renewal now this need for renewal was  something he knew not only in his  personal life but something that he  expressed in his music  as a matter of fact as I read many books  about him one of his great musicologists  who studied him extensively says st.  Florian is the perfect visual  representation of his music you actually  have a picture of the main nave on the  front of your bulletin and you can see  where he worshiped this writer talks  about the Magnificent baroque grandeur  that he knew you will hear something of  that grandeur in his music and talks  about the crypts and narrow passages in  the monastery this very haunted kind of  building and there is something haunting  about his music one critic elizabeth  meyer tries to summarize what Bruckner  is doing in his music and these are her  words she says he is attempting to  recover the lost spirituality of our  world attempting to recover the lost  spirituality of our world when I read  that it left right off the page I  thought it sounds like our age I think  of all the articles i've read about  people who are not religious but  spiritual this immense hunger that is in  the culture to recover a lost